{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9e4a141cc4620a91","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstatus: draft\n3\tspec: [005-bug-executor-restart-drops-deferred-respawn-queue]\n4\tcreated: \"2026-09-03T18:04:37Z\"\n5\tbranch: dark-factory/bug-executor-restart-drops-deferred-respawn-queue\n6\t---\n7\t\n8\t<summary>\n9\t- The per-assignee spawn lock in `spawnIfNeeded` is now taken unconditionally (for any resolved config) instead of only when `MaxConcurrentJobs > 0`, so the reconcile tick and the Kafka event path serialize count-then-spawn per assignee even for uncapped agents.\n10\t- Without this change an uncapped assignee could double-spawn the same task: both the reconcile loop and the Kafka consumer would read `IsJobActive == false` before either creates the Job, then both spawn.\n11\t- A new concurrency regression test races the reconcile path against the event path for the same uncapped task against a stateful fake spawner and asserts exactly one `SpawnJob`.\n12\t- This closes spec 005 AC 5 (\"no double-spawn\"); the reconcile loop from the previous prompt is what makes the race reachable for uncapped assignees, so this prompt runs after it.\n13\t</summary>\n14\t\n15\t<objective>\n16\tHarden `spawnIfNeeded` against the reconcile-vs-event double-spawn race by taking the per-assignee spawn lock unconditionally, and lock the behavior with a concurrent regression test that races both paths for the same uncapped task.\n17\t</objective>\n18\t\n19\t<context>\n20\tRead `/home/node/.claude/CLAUDE.md` for project conventions (Ginkgo/Gomega v2, `github.com/bborbe/errors` wrapping, counterfeiter mocks, coverage ≥80% for new code).\n21\t\n22\tRead these files fully before changing anything:\n23\t- `/workspace/pkg/handler/task_event_handler.go` — the `lockAssigneeSpawn` helper (lines 172-183) and the conditional lock in `spawnIfNeeded` (lines 466-473, the `if config != nil && config.MaxConcurrentJobs > 0` guard to change). The doc comment on `lockAssigneeSpawn` (lines 152-171) explains the serialization rationale — extend it to cover the uncapped case.\n24\t- `/workspace/pkg/handler/task_event_handler_test.go` — the existing concurrent test \"admits exactly MaxConcurrentJobs when spawns arrive concurrently\" (lines 305-361) for the capped case: note the `time.Sleep(20 * time.Millisecond)` widening pattern and the stateful `CountActiveJobs` stub. Mirror that technique.\n25\t- `/workspace/pkg/handler/task_reconcile_test.go` — created by the previous prompt; append the new regression spec here (do NOT extend the 1909-line `task_event_handler_test.go`; revive file-length-limit is 2000).\n26\t- `/workspace/pkg/spawner/job_spawner.go` — the `JobSpawner` interface (lines 39-52): `SpawnJob`, `IsJobActive`, `CountActiveJobs` — the fake used in the regression test.\n27\t\n28\tLibrary contracts (verified in this repo):\n29\t- `lib \"github.com/bborbe/agent\"` — `lib.Task`/`lib.TaskFrontmatter`; the fake `IsJobActive` reads `lib.TaskIdentifier`.\n30\t- `pkg.AgentConfiguration{Assignee: ..., Image: ..., MaxConcurrentJobs: 0}` — `MaxConcurrentJobs: 0` means uncapped (see `deferIfAtConcurrencyCap`, `task_event_handler.go:540`).\n31\t\n32\tRelevant docs (in-container paths):\n33\t- `/home/node/.claude/plugins/marketplaces/coding/docs/go-concurrency-patterns.md` — per-assignee lock rationale, no raw `go func` in production code (the test may use them with `GinkgoRecover`).\n34\t- `/home/node/.claude/plugins/marketplaces/coding/docs/go-testing-guide.md` — Ginkgo/Gomega concurrency assertions.\n35\t- `/home/node/.claude/plugins/marketplaces/coding/docs/go-error-wrapping-guide.md` — never `fmt.Errorf`.\n36\t- `/home/node/.claude/plugins/marketplaces/coding/docs/definition-of-done.md` — coverage rules for new code.\n37\t</context>\n38\t\n39\t<requirements>\n40\t1. In `/workspace/pkg/handler/task_event_handler.go`, change the conditional lock in `spawnIfNeeded` (lines 467-473) to be unconditional. Replace:\n41\t   ```go\n42\t   \t// Held across the cap check AND the SpawnJob call below, so no other goroutine\n43\t   \t// can observe a stale Job count in between. Only taken when a cap is configured:\n44\t   \t// an uncapped agent has nothing to serialize, and holding the lock anyway would\n45\t   \t// queue its spawns behind each other for no benefit.\n46\t   \tif config != nil && config.MaxConcurrentJobs > 0 {\n47\t   \t\tdefer h.lockAssigneeSpawn(config.Assignee)()\n48\t   \t}\n49\t   ```\n50\t   with:\n51\t   ```go\n52\t   \t// Held across the cap check AND the SpawnJob call below, so no other goroutine\n53\t   \t// can observe a stale Job count in between. Taken unconditionally (for any\n54\t   \t// resolved config, capped or not) because three goroutines reach spawnIfNeeded\n55\t   \t// — the Kafka consumer, the deferred-respawn loop, and the reconcile loop\n56\t   \t// (spec 005) — and for an UNCAPPED assignee they must still serialize\n57\t   \t// count-then-spawn so two of them cannot both read IsJobActive=false before\n58\t   \t// either creates the Job and double-spawn the same task. Spawns are rare\n59\t   \t// against Job runtimes measured in minutes, so the per-assignee serialization\n60\t   \t// costs nothing measurable. config is nil only for tasks that never reach this\n61\t   \t// code (empty assignee is filtered upstream); the nil guard stays defensive.\n62\t   \tif config != nil {\n63\t   \t\tdefer h.lockAssigneeSpawn(config.Assignee)()\n64\t   \t}\n65\t   ```\n66\t   Update the `lockAssigneeSpawn` doc comment (lines 152-171) to state that the lock now also serializes the reconcile loop against the event path for uncapped agents (keep the existing capped-race incident text; add one sentence: \"Taken unconditionally since spec 005: the reconcile loop and the Kafka consumer must also serialize for uncapped assignees, or a reconcile tick racing the event path for the same task would double-spawn.\")\n67\t\n68\t2. Append one regression spec to `/workspace/pkg/handler/task_reconcile_test.go` (inside the existing `Describe(\"TaskEventHandler reconcile loop\", ...)` block). It races the event path and the reconcile path for the SAME uncapped task and asserts exactly one `SpawnJob`:\n69\t   ```go\n70\t   It(\"races the reconcile path against the Kafka event path for an uncapped task and spawns exactly one job (AC 5)\", func() {\n71\t       // Spec 005 AC 5: with the per-assignee lock taken unconditionally, a\n72\t       // reconcile tick racing the event path for the SAME uncapped task results\n73\t       // in exactly one Job. Without the unconditional lock both goroutines read\n74\t       // IsJobActive=false before either has created its Job, then both spawn.\n75\t       // The fake is stateful like the real cluster: IsJobActive reports whether a\n76\t       // Job has been created, and the sleep widens the read->spawn window so the\n77\t       // unlocked race is deterministic rather than luck-of-the-timing.\n78\t       fakeResolver.ResolveReturns(\n79\t           pkg.AgentConfiguration{Assignee: \"claude\", Image: \"my-image:latest\", MaxConcurrentJobs: 0},\n80\t           nil,\n81\t       )\n82\t       var spawned atomic.Int64\n83\t       fakeSpawner.CountActiveJobsReturns(0, nil)\n84\t       fakeSpawner.SpawnJobStub = func(\n85\t           _ context.Context,\n86\t           _ lib.Task,\n87\t           _ pkg.AgentConfiguration,\n88\t       ) (string, error) {\n89\t           spawned.Add(1)\n90\t           return \"job-name\", nil\n91\t       }\n92\t       fakeSpawner.IsJobActiveStub = func(\n93\t           _ context.Context,\n94\t           _ lib.TaskIdentifier,\n95\t       ) (bool, error) {\n96\t           time.Sleep(20 * time.Millisecond)\n97\t           return spawned.Load() > 0, nil\n98\t       }\n99\t       fakeGitRestClient.IsReadyReturns(true, nil)\n100\t\n101\t       task := lib.Task{\n102\t           TaskIdentifier: lib.TaskIdentifier(\"tid-double-spawn\"),\n103\t           Frontmatter: lib.TaskFrontmatter{\n104\t               \"status\":   \"in_progress\",\n105\t               \"phase\":    string(domain.TaskPhaseExecution),\n106\t               \"assignee\": \"claude\",\n107\t               \"stage\":    \"prod\",\n108\t           },\n109\t       }\n110\t       fakeGitRestClient.ListReturns([]string{\"24 Tasks/tid-double-spawn.md\"}, nil)\n111\t       fakeGitRestClient.GetReturns([]byte(renderTaskFile(task)), nil)\n112\t\n113\t       var wg sync.WaitGroup\n114\t       wg.Add(2)\n115\t       go func() {\n116\t           defer GinkgoRecover()\n117\t           defer wg.Done()\n118\t           Expect(h.ConsumeMessage(ctx, buildMsg(task))).To(BeNil())\n119\t       }()\n120\t       go func() {\n121\t           defer GinkgoRecover()\n122\t           defer wg.Done()\n123\t           Expect(h.ReconcileOnce(ctx)).To(BeNil())\n124\t       }()\n125\t       wg.Wait()\n126\t\n127\t       Expect(fakeSpawner.SpawnJobCallCount()).To(Equal(1))\n128\t   })\n129\t   ```\n130\t   This spec needs `buildMsg` (defined in the existing `task_event_handler_test.go` in the same `handler_test` package) and `renderTaskFile` (defined in `task_reconcile_test.go`). Imports `sync`, `sync/atomic`, `time` already in the reconcile test file if not present.\n131\t\n132\t3. Do NOT add a CHANGELOG entry in this prompt — the single `feat:` bullet in `## Unreleased` from the previous prompt already covers the double-spawn hardening (its text names the unconditional lock). Do NOT add any other code changes: no new config, no metrics, no interface changes.\n133\t</requirements>\n134\t\n135\t<constraints>\n136\t- Repo conventions are frozen: Ginkgo/Gomega v2 tests, `github.com/bborbe/errors` wrapping, counterfeiter mocks, coverage ≥80% for new code.\n137\t- The lock becomes unconditional for any resolved config (`config != nil`); the `config == nil` path stays unlocked (no assignee to serialize on — such tasks never reach this code).\n138\t- Do NOT change the Kafka event path, `deferIfAtConcurrencyCap`, or `checkActiveCurrentJob` — only the lock guard and its comments.\n139\t- Do NOT make the interval, the lock, or anything else configurable — the spec forbids new knobs.\n140\t- Put the regression spec in `pkg/handler/task_reconcile_test.go` — NEVER in the 1909-line `task_event_handler_test.go` (revive file-length-limit is 2000).\n141\t- Do NOT commit — dark-factory handles git.\n142\t- Existing tests must still pass (including the existing capped concurrent test at `task_event_handler_test.go:305` — with the lock now always taken, its behavior is unchanged because the lock was already taken for capped configs).\n143\t</constraints>\n144\t\n145\t<verification>\n146\tRun `go test -mod=mod ./pkg/handler/...` iteratively after the change (fast feedback loop), then `make precommit` ONCE at the very end.\n147\t\n148\t- `go test -mod=mod ./pkg/handler/...` — exits 0.\n149\t- `make precommit` — exits 0.\n150\t- `grep -n 'lockAssigneeSpawn' pkg/handler/task_event_handler.go` — shows the unconditional `if config != nil` guard in `spawnIfNeeded` (acceptance-criterion evidence: \"the per-assignee spawn lock is taken unconditionally in spawnIfNeeded\").\n151\t- `go test -mod=mod -run TestHandler -count=10 ./pkg/handler/` — the double-spawn regression spec passes across repeated runs (the race is timing-sensitive; -count=10 makes flakiness visible).\n152\t- `go test -mod=mod -coverprofile=/tmp/cover.out ./pkg/handler/... && go tool cover -func=/tmp/cover.out` — the modified `spawnIfNeeded` branch is exercised.\n153\t\n154\tDo NOT run `docker`, `make build`, `kubectl`, `dark-factory`, `gh`, or `scripts/*.sh` commands in this prompt — those are operator-executable and belong on the spec's verification ladder.\n155\t</verification>\n156\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9be1dac94caaf56f","session_id":"3667b84f-c892-4ca5-8115-a830204bf3b3","uuid":"27b0ea1c-ba30-440e-9486-dc8063cd2224","timestamp":"2026-09-03T18:08:18.229Z","subagent_type":"dark-factory:prompt-auditor","task_description":"Audit prompt 3 spawn-lock"}
