{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"a45d3f8651a5729e1","tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9be1dac94caaf56f","status":"completed","output_file":"/tmp/claude-1000/-workspace/3667b84f-c892-4ca5-8115-a830204bf3b3/tasks/a45d3f8651a5729e1.output","summary":"I have everything I need. Here is the audit report.\n\n---\n\n# Prompt Audit Report: Unconditional per-assignee spawn lock + double-spawn regression test (spec 005, prompt 3)\n\n**File**: `/workspace/prompts/3-spec-005-spawn-lock.md`\n**Score**: 7/10\n**Status**: Needs Improvement (single Critical must-fix; everything else verified)\n\n## DoD Checklist\n- [x] `<summary>` present — 5 bullets, behavior-focused\n- [x] `<objective>` states end state (harden `spawnIfNeeded` + lock behavior with a regression test)\n- [x] `<context>` present and specific\n- [x] `<requirements>` numbered and specific, with exact old→new snippets\n- [x] `<constraints>` present, includes \"Do NOT commit — dark-factory handles git\"\n- [x] `<verification>` present with runnable container commands\n- [x] All paths valid in-container (`/workspace/...` = mounted project root; `/home/node/.claude/...` = coding plugin + global CLAUDE.md, both real mount targets). No `~/` or host-absolute paths. No flag.\n- [x] No bare `git ` commands in `<verification>`; `.dark-factory.yaml` is `workflow: direct` with no `hideGit` (no masking). PASS.\n- [x] No operator-only commands in `<verification>` (`docker`, `make build`, `make buca`, `kubectl*`, `gh`, `scripts/*.sh`, `dark-factory`). The prompt even names and forbids them explicitly. PASS.\n- [x] File in `prompts/` inbox, not `prompts/in-progress/`. PASS.\n- [x] Filename `3-spec-005-spawn-lock.md` matches the multi-prompt ordering convention `^[1-9]-spec-\\d{3}-.*\\.md$`; no dark-factory global prefix. PASS.\n- [x] Filename reflects the primary change (the spawn lock). PASS.\n- [x] Status is `draft`. PASS.\n\n## Failure Mode Coverage\n- [x] Spec 005 Failure Modes table cross-checked. The \"Reconcile and Kafka event race for the same task → exactly one Job — per-assignee lock serializes\" row is exactly what this prompt's regression test asserts. The other rows (git-rest 503/rate-limit/slow, live Job, over-cap, config unresolvable, restart mid-reconcile) are covered by sibling prompt 2 (`2-spec-005-reconcile-loop.md`, specs f–k). Every trigger has a matching requirement across the batch. PASS.\n- [x] Security section: no new listeners, git-rest read-only, gateway secret by name, Config-CR gate — handled by prompts 1/2; prompt 3 touches no security surface. N/A.\n- [x] External calls: N/A — the change is a lock guard + a test; no new external calls introduced.\n- [x] Boundary-crossing contract test: the regression spec is a level-2 integration test — it races the real production paths (`h.ConsumeMessage` and `h.ReconcileOnce`, both reaching the real `spawnIfNeeded`) through a stateful counterfeiter fake, asserting exactly one `SpawnJob`. This crosses the real dispatch boundary (not a shape test). PASS.\n- [x] No `go mod vendor` (repo has no `vendor/`; verification correctly uses `-mod=mod`). PASS.\n- [x] No cross-repo writes (no clone/push/gh). PASS.\n- [x] Container-autonomous: no `kubectl`, no deploy/rollout, no operator-vs-agent split. PASS.\n- [x] Sibling entry points: the change is inside the single shared `spawnIfNeeded` (callers verified at `task_event_handler.go:212` event path and `:602` deferred-respawn loop; the reconcile loop calls it too). All entry points inherit the unconditional lock from the one edit — no parallel implementation to update. PASS.\n\n## Documentation Placement\n- [x] No inlined reusable pattern (>10 lines of generic coding knowledge). The ~60-line test spec is the change's own artifact, and it explicitly mirrors the existing technique at `task_event_handler_test.go:305` rather than duplicating pattern knowledge.\n- [x] Project/coding docs referenced in `<context>` (go-concurrency-patterns, go-testing-guide, go-error-wrapping-guide, definition-of-done) rather than inlined.\n- [x] No knowledge inlined that outlives the prompt.\n\n## Code Reference Verification\nAll line references verified by reading the files:\n\n| Reference | Status |\n|-----------|--------|\n| `task_event_handler.go:152-171` doc comment on `lockAssigneeSpawn` | Correct (152-171 = doc, function at 172-183) |\n| `task_event_handler.go:466-473` / `:467-473` conditional lock guard (`if config != nil && config.MaxConcurrentJobs > 0` at line 471) — old text in req 1 matches verbatim | Correct |\n| `task_event_handler.go:540` `deferIfAtConcurrencyCap` `config == nil \\|\\| MaxConcurrentJobs <= 0` → \"0 = uncapped\" | Correct |\n| `task_event_handler_test.go:305-361` existing capped concurrent test (`time.Sleep(20ms)` widening, `CountActiveJobsStub`) | Correct |\n| `job_spawner.go:39-52` `JobSpawner` interface (`SpawnJob`, `IsJobActive`, `CountActiveJobs`) | Correct |\n| `buildMsg` defined in `task_event_handler_test.go` | **STALE/WRONG scope — see Critical Issue 1** |\n| `renderTaskFile` defined in `task_reconcile_test.go` (sibling prompt 2, req 10, same Describe block) | Correct — in scope |\n| `domain.TaskPhaseExecution` (vault-cli v0.121.0 `pkg/domain/task_phase.go:23`) | Correct |\n| Mock signatures (`IsJobActiveStub`, `SpawnJobStub`, `CountActiveJobsReturns`, `ListReturns`, `GetReturns`, `IsReadyReturns`) all match counterfeiter-generated signatures on `mocks.FakeJobSpawner` / `mocks.FakeGitRestClient` | Correct |\n\nNote: `task_reconcile_test.go` does not exist yet — it is created by sibling prompt 2, and this prompt's summary correctly states it runs after prompt 2. Batch ordering dependency is acknowledged and consistent.\n\n## Coding Guidelines Compliance\n| Guideline | Status |\n|-----------|--------|\n| Test framework: Ginkgo/Gomega v2 + counterfeiter fakes (no hand-written mocks, no stdlib table tests) | Compliant |\n| Error wrapping: no `fmt.Errorf`; test uses `errors.Errorf(ctx,...)`-free happy path | Compliant |\n| Concurrency: raw `go func` only in the test, with `defer GinkgoRecover()` — prompt explicitly notes production code must not use it | Compliant |\n| Test-only package-level mutable state: none introduced | Compliant |\n| Coverage ≥80% for new code: new branch exercised by the regression test; verification runs `-coverprofile` | Compliant |\n\n## YAGNI Pass (scope-creep detector)\n- [x] No opt-out flag / escape hatch that negates the Goal.\n- [x] No tunable threshold or config field added — constraints explicitly forbid new knobs (\"Do NOT make the interval, the lock, or anything else configurable — the spec forbids new knobs\").\n- [x] No future-proofing branches. Req 3 explicitly forbids extra code (no config, no metrics, no interface changes, no CHANGELOG).\n- Scope matches spec DB 4 / AC 5 exactly. PASS.\n\n## Critical Issues\n\n**Critical Issue 1 — `buildMsg` is not visible from the new spec's Describe block; the regression test will not compile as written.**\n\nRequirement 2 says: \"This spec needs `buildMsg` (defined in the existing `task_event_handler_test.go` in the same `handler_test` package)\".\n\nThat is factually wrong. `buildMsg` is declared at `task_event_handler_test.go:73` as a **closure-local variable inside `var _ = Describe(\"TaskEventHandler\", ...)`** (the anonymous function passed to `Describe` at line 41). Being in the same package does not give another file's Describe block access to a closure-local. The new spec is appended inside `Describe(\"TaskEventHandler reconcile loop\", ...)` in `task_reconcile_test.go` — a different closure in a different file — so `buildMsg` is out of scope there. Referencing it produces `undefined: buildMsg`, a compile failure in the very test this prompt ships.\n\nFind/replace:\n\nOLD:\n```\nThis spec needs `buildMsg` (defined in the existing `task_event_handler_test.go` in the same `handler_test` package) and `renderTaskFile` (defined in `task_reconcile_test.go`). Imports `sync`, `sync/atomic`, `time` already in the reconcile test file if not present.\n```\n\nNEW:\n```\nThis spec needs `renderTaskFile` (defined in the same `Describe(\"TaskEventHandler reconcile loop\", ...)` block) and a `buildMsg` helper. `buildMsg` is declared at `task_event_handler_test.go:73` as a closure-local inside that file's `Describe(\"TaskEventHandler\", ...)` block and is NOT visible from this file's separate Describe block — define a local `buildMsg` at the top of this Describe block, mirroring `task_event_handler_test.go:73` verbatim (json.Marshal the task into `&sarama.ConsumerMessage{Value: value}`). Ensure imports `sync`, `sync/atomic`, `time`, `encoding/json`, and `github.com/IBM/sarama` are present (prompt 2's import list for this file does not include `sync`/`sync/atomic`/`time`/`encoding/json`/`sarama`).\n```\n\n## Recommendations\n\n1. **Req 2 import phrasing (part of the fix above):** \"Imports `sync`, `sync/atomic`, `time` already in the reconcile test file if not present\" is self-contradictory (\"already ... if not present\") and is factually wrong for this file — sibling prompt 2's import list for `task_reconcile_test.go` does not include any of `sync`, `sync/atomic`, `time`. Make it an imperative: \"Ensure imports ... are present.\"\n\n2. **Summary contains identifiers:** `<summary>` bullets 1 and 3 name `spawnIfNeeded`, `MaxConcurrentJobs`, `SpawnJob`. The DoD asks for plain language with no function/struct names. The bullets still read as observable outcomes, so this is a style nit — consider rewording bullet 1 (\"the per-assignee spawn lock is taken for every resolved config, capped or not...\") and bullet 3 (\"a concurrency regression test ... asserts exactly one job is spawned\").\n\n3. **`branch:` frontmatter field:** the DoD allows only `spec`, `status`, `created`, `issue` in inbox frontmatter; this file (and all 7 sibling prompts across both batches) carries `branch:`. It is uniform generator convention, so not a blocker — flagging only for strictness; drop it if the 